### Detailed Technical Study: ArrayList vs. LinkedList with Scanner Integration

#### 1. Internal Memory Mechanics:
- **`ArrayList`:** Uses a contiguous memory buffer. When capacity is exceeded, it creates a new array of size $1.5 \times$ old capacity and copies all elements. Index lookup `get(i)` is a simple memory offset calculation ($O(1)$).
- **`LinkedList`:** Uses discrete heap nodes where each node contains `prev`, `data`, and `next` references. Inserting or removing from the head or tail requires zero element shifting ($O(1)$).

#### 2. Algorithmic Complexity Comparison:
| Operation | `ArrayList` Complexity | `LinkedList` Complexity |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Get by Index (`get(i)`)** | **$O(1)$** | **$O(n)$** |
| **Insert at End (`add(e)`)** | Amortized $O(1)$ | $O(1)$ |
| **Insert at Middle (`add(i, e)`)**| $O(n)$ (array shift) | $O(n)$ (traversal to index) + $O(1)$ insertion |
| **Memory Consumption** | Lower (compact array) | Higher (2 reference pointers per node) |

#### 3. University Problem Solution: Read Data via Scanner & Store into LinkedList:
```java
import java.util.*;

public class LinkedListScannerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // String input simulating student names
        String inputData = "Alice Bob Charlie David Emily";
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(inputData);

        // Store into LinkedList
        LinkedList<String> studentList = new LinkedList<>();

        while (scanner.hasNext()) {
            studentList.add(scanner.next());
        }
        scanner.close();

        System.out.println("=== Stored LinkedList Elements ===");
        System.out.println("Complete List:   " + studentList);
        System.out.println("First Student:   " + studentList.getFirst());
        System.out.println("Last Student:    " + studentList.getLast());

        // Demonstrate efficient insertion at head and tail
        studentList.addFirst("Zack (Transferred In)");
        studentList.addLast("Zoe (Late Admission)");

        System.out.println("\nAfter addFirst/addLast Operations:");
        for (String name : studentList) {
            System.out.println("  * " + name);
        }
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{ArrayList maximizes cache locality for read operations; LinkedList eliminates array reallocation pauses.}$$
